###["Here are three possible feature engineering steps to improve the downstream task of classifying vehicle silhouettes into four types:\n\n---\n\npython\n# Column: COMPACTNESS_SCALED\n# Feature description: Z-score normalized version of the COMPACTNESS feature to remove scale effects.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing a highly dispersed feature like COMPACTNESS ensures it contributes proportionately during model training. Normalization can improve optimization and model convergence, especially for algorithms sensitive to feature scale like logistic regression or gradient descent-based models.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_SCALED'] = (df['COMPACTNESS'] - df['COMPACTNESS'].mean()) / df['COMPACTNESS'].std()\nend\n\npython\n# Column: MAJOR_MINOR_ASPECT_RATIO\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Provides insight into the elongation or symmetry of the silhouette. A high value might correspond to very elongated features (e.g., VANs), while a lower value might signify more symmetric silhouettes (e.g., BUS). This feature could help separate distinct shape types.\ndf['MAJOR_MINOR_ASPECT_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']\nend\n\npython\n# Column: RECTANGULARITY_DIFFERENCE\n# Feature description: Difference between PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY and MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY.\n# Usefulness: By comparing these two rectangularity features, we can evaluate how closely aligned the minor/principal axes are with the maximum boundary dimensions. It may help in distinguishing vehicles like OPEL (which might have tighter, compact shapes) from elongated ones like VANs.\ndf['RECTANGULARITY_DIFFERENCE'] = df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'] - df['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY']\nend\n\n---\n\nFor dropping non-contributory or redundant features:\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Reason: This feature has a very tight range (0–22) and relatively low variation (Std = 5.0072). It is less likely to contribute significant variability to the model. Additionally, its utility in distinguishing vehicle types based on shape is unclear.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: HOLLOWS_RATIO has a very narrow range (181–211) and low standard deviation, so it might not carry enough informative variability for distinguishing classes. Additionally, bounding polygon areas are already indirectly captured through other surface area-related features like COMPACTNESS and RADIUS_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: The feature MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO, due to its low interquartile range (Q3 - Q1 = 3), provides limited information diversity. It overlaps conceptually with RADIUS_RATIO and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, which are likely better indicators of silhouette elongation.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThe new features aim to amplify shape-related properties (e.g., symmetry, elongation) and normalize scales to improve model utility, while the dropped features remove redundant or non-contributory noise.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY. Represents the relationship between compactness and circularity, potentially capturing distinguishing geometric shapes for different vehicles.\n# Usefulness: This might highlight differences in vehicle class silhouettes, as certain vehicle types might have a stronger or weaker relationship between compactness and circularity.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['CIRCULARITY']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: SKEWNESS_KURTOSIS_DIFFERENCE\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS and KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS. Indicates how symmetric and heavy-tailed the shape distribution is across the major and minor axes.\n# Usefulness: Greater differences might correspond to vehicle types where one axis exhibits higher elongation or asymmetry, potentially aiding in classification.\ndf['SKEWNESS_KURTOSIS_DIFFERENCE'] = abs(df['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS'] - df['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: ELONGATEDNESS_SCATTER_RATIO_PRODUCT\n# Feature description: The product of ELONGATEDNESS and SCATTER_RATIO. Captures how vehicle shape elongation interacts with the scatter distribution of mass about axes.\n# Usefulness: Products of key geometric ratios might help identify vehicles where the interplay of elongation and scatter is a defining feature.\ndf['ELONGATEDNESS_SCATTER_RATIO_PRODUCT'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] * df['SCATTER_RATIO']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This column has a very large range ([191, 1018]) compared to other features, and its distribution shows high skew (Mean = 449.1913, Std = 179.8980). It may not provide robust discriminative information or could dominate other features while training the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# The KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS column likely overlaps in interpretative utility with other kurtosis/skewness-related features. Its removal simplifies feature space without losing significant information.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY might offer redundant information already captured by a combination of features like MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY and other shape aspect ratios, reducing risks of multicollinearity.\ndf.drop(columns=['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
