ariation (Std = 5.0072). It is less likely to contribute significant variability to the model. Additionally, its utility in distinguishing vehicle types based on shape is unclear.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: HOLLOWS_RATIO has a very narrow range (181–211) and low standard deviation, so it might not carry enough informative variability for distinguishing classes. Additionally, bounding polygon areas are already indirectly captured through other surface area-related features like COMPACTNESS and RADIUS_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: The feature MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO, due to its low interquartile range (Q3 - Q1 = 3), provides limited information diversity. It overlaps conceptually with RADIUS_RATIO and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO, which are likely better indicators of silhouette elongation.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThe new features aim to amplify shape-related properties (e.g., symmetry, elongation) and normalize scales to improve model utility, while the dropped features remove redundant or non-contributory noise.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY. Represents the relationship between compactness and circularity, potentially capturing distinguishing geometric shapes for different vehicles.\n# Usefulness: This might highlight differences in vehicle class silhouettes, as certain vehicle types might have a stronger or weaker relationship between compactness and circularity.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['CIRCULARITY']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: SKEWNESS_KURTOSIS_DIFFERENCE\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS and KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS. Indicates how symmetric and heavy-tailed the shape distribution is across the major and minor axes.\n# Usefulness: Greater differences might correspond to vehicle types where one axis exhibits higher elongation or asymmetry, potentially aiding in classification.\ndf['SKEWNESS_KURTOSIS_DIFFERENCE'] = abs(df['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS'] - df['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: ELONGATEDNESS_SCATTER_RATIO_PRODUCT\n# Feature description: The product of ELONGATEDNESS and SCATTER_RATIO. Captures how vehicle shape elongation interacts with the scatter distribution of mass about axes.\n# Usefulness: Products of key geometric ratios might help identify vehicles where the interplay of elongation and scatter is a defining feature.\ndf['ELONGATEDNESS_SCATTER_RATIO_PRODUCT'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] * df['SCATTER_RATIO']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This column has a very large range ([191, 1018]) compared to other features, and its distribution shows high skew (Mean = 449.1913, Std = 179.8980). It may not provide robust discriminative information or could dominate other features while training the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# The KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS column likely overlaps in interpretative utility with other kurtosis/skewness-related features. Its removal simplifies feature space without losing significant information.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY might offer redundant information already captured by a combination of features like MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY and other shape aspect ratios, reducing risks of multicollinearity.\ndf.drop(columns=['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible next steps to generate new features to improve vehicle classification:\n\n1. Feature Engineering: RADIUS_RATIO_SCALED\npython\n# RADIUS_RATIO_SCALED\n# Feature description: Z-score normalized version of the RADIUS_RATIO feature to remove scale effects and standardize values.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing a feature with significant variability (Std = 33.7833) ensures it contributes proportionately in the model, mitigating scale effects. This can improve optimization in algorithms sensitive to feature scaling, like gradient-based classifiers.\ndf['RADIUS_RATIO_SCALED'] = (df['RADIUS_RATIO'] - df['RADIUS_RATIO'].mean()) / df['RADIUS_RATIO'].std()\nend\n\n2. Feature Engineering: DISTANCE_SHAPE_RATIO\npython\n# DISTANCE_SHAPE_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY to COMPACTNESS, capturing how distribution of border distances relates to silhouette compactness.\n# Usefulness: Vehicles with similar compactness may differ in border distance distribution, making this ratio useful for distinguishing vehicle types with similar overall size but different shapes (e.g., OPEL vs. VAN).\ndf['DISTANCE_SHAPE_RATIO'] = df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'] / df['COMPACTNESS']\nend\n\n3. Feature Engineering: MOMENT_SUM_NORMALIZED\npython\n# MOMENT_SUM_NORMALIZED\n# Feature description: Normalized sum of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS, scaled by total area.\n# Usefulness: Captures overall distribution of moments across the major and minor axes relative to area, helping to differentiate highly elongated vehicles like VANs from compact shapes like OPEL or SAAB.\ndf['MOMENT_SUM_NORMALIZED'] = (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']) / df['COMPACTNESS']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping columns that are likely redundant or non-contributory:\n\n1. Dropping SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Reason: This feature has a very tight range (0–22) and low standard deviation (Std = 5.0072), meaning limited variability. Its utility in distinguishing vehicle types appears minimal compared to more dispersed features. \ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n2. Dropping HOLLOWS_RATIO\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: HOLLOWS_RATIO has a narrow range ([181–211]) and a low standard deviation (Std = 7.3390), indicating limited utility for classification. Other area-related features (e.g., COMPACTNESS, ELONGATEDNESS) likely capture similar information more effectively.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n3. Dropping PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY\npython\n# Explanation why the column PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# Reason: PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY overlaps with features like MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY in terms of capturing rectangular area alignment. Removing it simplifies feature space and reduces risks of multicollinearity.\ndf.drop(columns=['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Summary:\n- Generated features capture new interactions and normalized interpretations of existing features.\n- Dropped features were chosen based on their limited variability or overlap with other more explanatory features.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY. Highlights the relationship between compactness and how uniformly the silhouette's area is distributed relative to its border distances.\n# Usefulness: This composite feature could capture nuanced differences between vehicle types, particularly those with compact shapes (e.g., OPEL) versus more spread-out ones (e.g., VAN).\ndf['COMPACTNESS_DISTANCE_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: ASPECT_RATIO_DIFFERENCE\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO and MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO. Shows the discrepancy between the proportionality of specific axes.\n# Usefulness: This difference could be useful in emphasizing contrasts in shape proportionality, potentially delineating vehicles that have more elongated or symmetric silhouettes based on axis variance.\ndf['ASPECT_RATIO_DIFFERENCE'] = abs(df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] - df['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'])\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: ELONGATION_RECTANGULARITY_INTERACTION\n# Feature description: The product of ELONGATEDNESS and MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY. Encodes how elongation interacts with the rectangularity of the maximum bounding dimensions.\n# Usefulness: By combining these two traits, this feature may highlight specific geometric tendencies in silhouettes, helping classify between more stretched vehicles (e.g., VAN) versus compact ones like OPEL.\ndf['ELONGATION_RECTANGULARITY_INTERACTION'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] * df['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY']\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Reason: SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has a narrow range (0–22) and low variance (Std = 5.0072), making it less likely to contribute significant discriminative power for the classification task.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: HOLLOWS_RATIO has a very narrow range (181–211) and low standard deviation (Std = 7.3390). The feature's limited variability reduces its usefulness in identifying unique vehicle classes.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a small interquartile range (Q3 - Q1 = 3). It likely overlaps conceptually with other features related to shape ratios, such as RADIUS_RATIO and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering aimed at improving vehicle silhouette classification:\n\n### Adding Columns:\npython\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_TO_RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to RADIUS_RATIO. Captures the relationship between shape compactness and radius variability, potentially differentiating vehicles with tight silhouettes (like OPEL or SAAB) from those with elongated features (like VAN or BUS).\n# Usefulness: This feature combines two key shape properties to emphasize extreme differences in compactness and radius fluctuations, improving classification performance.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_TO_RADIUS_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['RADIUS_RATIO']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: SHAPE_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: The product of SCATTER_RATIO and DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY. Encodes how mass distribution interacts with area distribution based on average border distance.\n# Usefulness: By combining scatter and circularity, this feature helps differentiate between evenly distributed shapes (e.g., compact vehicles like OPEL) versus irregular or elongated ones (e.g., BUS or VAN).\ndf['SHAPE_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] * df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: SYMMETRY_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS. Highlights the relative elongation and symmetry along major and minor axes.\n# Usefulness: This feature helps identify how symmetric or elongated the silhouette is. High symmetry ratios may indicate more uniform shapes (e.g., SAAB or BUS), while lower ratios could imply stronger elongation (e.g., VAN).\ndf['SYMMETRY_RATIO'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] / df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']\nend\n\n### Dropping Columns:\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has a very narrow range (0–22) and low standard deviation (Std = 5.0072). This limits its variability, making it less likely to contribute meaningful discriminative power across vehicle classes.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO has a narrow range (181–211) with low standard deviation (Std = 7.3390), suggesting limited variability. Other area-related features (e.g., ELONGATEDNESS or COMPACTNESS) are likely more effective at capturing similar information.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a low interquartile range (Q3 - Q1 = 3) and overlaps conceptually with other features like RADIUS_RATIO and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO. Removing it simplifies the feature space without losing significant information.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Summary:\nThe added features combine existing attributes to capture meaningful geometric and distributional interactions that are likely to improve classification performance. Dropped columns are chosen based on low variability and conceptual redundancy, simplifying the feature space.", "python\n# Feature: COMPACTNESS_TO_RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to RADIUS_RATIO. Highlights how compactness relates to the variability in radius across the silhouette.\n# Usefulness: Vehicles with similar compactness may have varied radius ratios, distinguishing more circular shapes (e.g., BUS) from elongated shapes (e.g., VAN).\ndf['COMPACTNESS_TO_RADIUS_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['RADIUS_RATIO']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: ELONGATION_TO_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of ELONGATEDNESS to SCATTER_RATIO. Captures how the elongation of the silhouette interacts with the distribution of mass.\n# Usefulness: This combination may help differentiate stretched vehicles (e.g., VAN) from more compact or symmetric ones (e.g., OPEL or BUS).\ndf['ELONGATION_TO_SCATTER_RATIO'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] / df['SCATTER_RATIO']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature: MAJOR_MINOR_MOMENT_DIFFERENCE\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS. Indicates the asymmetry in mass distribution along the major and minor axes.\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights significant asymmetry in how the silhouette's mass is distributed, which may correlate with specific vehicle shapes (e.g., VANs being more asymmetric).\ndf['MAJOR_MINOR_MOMENT_DIFFERENCE'] = abs(df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] - df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'])\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# Reason: This column has a very narrow range (0–22) and low standard deviation (Std = 5.0072), meaning it provides limited variability. This minor variability reduces its impact on differentiating vehicle types.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: HOLLOWS_RATIO has a narrow range (181–211) and low standard deviation (Std = 7.3390). This feature adds limited diversity relative to other area-based metrics, likely contributing little to the classification task.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# Reason: MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a very small interquartile range (Q3 - Q1 = 3), indicating low variability. It overlaps conceptually with more informative features like PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO and RADIUS_RATIO.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
